# `read-path`, not `get`. The public wrapper (code/environment/path.tree) declares `get`, and names are
# PACKAGE-GLOBAL in Term, so a public task and its native implementation sharing a name unify into one symbol:
# wiring them emitted `function get1(kind) { return get1(kind) }`, an infinite recursion. That is why this module
# and twelve like it declared an API and bound nothing for six months. The convention that works is the one
# code/environment.tree already uses, where the native names are `current-directory` and `get-variable` and cannot
# collide with the `directory` and `variable` they implement.
#
# THE ARMS WERE MALFORMED TOO, and that is the reason the first fix was not enough. Every arm was written
#
#     hook
#       test
#       call is-equal ...
#       send back ...
#
# with a bare `hook` and `test` as a child. An arm is `hook test` with the condition under it and the body under a
# nested `hook hold` (deck/mill/code/code/fork/mine.tree), so the grammar matched no arm kind and the whole chain
# emitted `if (kind == "home") {} else if (kind == "temporary") {}` -- every branch empty, every body dropped, no
# diagnostic. The module compiled, shipped, and returned nothing for six months.
#
# THERE IS NO FINAL ARM. A `fork roll` is a chain of GUARDS: each arm is `hook test` with its condition, and its
# body under a nested `hook hold`. Neither `hook else` nor `hook miss` builds at the top of a roll -- both fail with
# `the mint bridge does not build the send expression yet`, pointing at the body, because the arm's first child is
# read as a condition. The fallthrough is a statement AFTER the fork, which is what the shape means anyway: the
# guards return, and what follows is the default. The original wrote `hook else`, which is one more reason this
# module never ran.

load @term/seed/code/path
  find join

# `dock load`, not a `load` of @term/bind. The bind package declares the SHAPE of node's `os` (`task homedir,
# name <homedir>`) and imports nothing, so calling through it emits `throw new Error("stub: homedir")`. The import
# is a dock, which is what code/native/node/environment/language.tree already does.
dock load
  load <node:os>, name os
  load <node:process>, name node-process

# One well-known directory of this environment, by name. `home` and `temporary` come straight from the platform;
# the other three follow the XDG base directory specification on Linux and the platform convention elsewhere.
task read-path
  take kind, like text
  like text
  fork roll
    hook test
      call is-equal
        read kind
        text <home>
      hook hold
        send back
          call os/homedir
    hook test
      call is-equal
        read kind
        text <temporary>
      hook hold
        send back
          call os/tmpdir
    hook test
      call is-equal
        read kind
        text <data>
      hook hold
        send back
          call read-kind
            read kind
            text <Library/Application Support>
            text <APPDATA>
            text <XDG_DATA_HOME>
            text <.local/share>
    hook test
      call is-equal
        read kind
        text <configuration>
      hook hold
        send back
          call read-kind
            read kind
            text <Library/Preferences>
            text <APPDATA>
            text <XDG_CONFIG_HOME>
            text <.config>
    hook test
      call is-equal
        read kind
        text <cache>
      hook hold
        send back
          call read-kind
            read kind
            text <Library/Caches>
            text <LOCALAPPDATA>
            text <XDG_CACHE_HOME>
            text <.cache>

  # every other name: the home directory, which is always a real path
  send back
    call os/homedir

# The three directories that differ by platform, in one place rather than three copies of the same fork. `mac` is
# the suffix under the user's home on darwin, `windows` the environment variable to read there, `slot` the XDG
# variable on everything else and `fall` the path under the home when that variable is unset.
task read-kind
  take kind, like text
  take mac, like text
  take windows, like text
  take slot, like text
  take fall, like text
  like text
  save system
    call os/platform
  fork roll
    hook test
      call is-equal
        read system
        text <darwin>
      hook hold
        save home
          call os/homedir
        send back
          call join
            read home
            read mac
    hook test
      call is-equal
        read system
        text <win32>
      hook hold
        send back
          call read-slot
            read windows

  # everything else follows the XDG base directory specification: the variable when it is set, the documented
  # path under the home directory when it is not
  save xdg
    call read-slot
      read slot
  # an unset variable reads as the empty text, not as void: `read-slot` answers `like text` either way
  fork test
    call is-unequal
      read xdg
      text <>
    hook hold
      send back, read xdg
  save home
    call os/homedir
  send back
    call join
      read home
      read fall

# One environment variable, by name
task read-slot
  take name, like text
  like text
  send back
    read node-process/env/{name}
